12:["$","$L29",null,{"faqItems":[{"q":"When should I see an ENT specialist for hoarseness?","a":"Occasional hoarseness may occur with a cold, voice overuse or throat irritation. Persistent, recurrent or unexplained hoarseness should be assessed by an ENT specialist, particularly when it does not improve, keeps returning, or is associated with swallowing difficulty, breathing problems, neck swelling, pain or coughing blood."},{"q":"What causes a hoarse or changed voice?","a":"Common causes include laryngitis, vocal strain, vocal cord nodules or polyps, reflux-related irritation, allergies and smoking. Less common causes include vocal cord paralysis, neurological disorders and laryngeal growths. The cause should be identified before treatment is selected."},{"q":"How are vocal cord problems diagnosed?","a":"Depending on the symptoms, an ENT examination may include flexible or rigid laryngoscopy, video laryngoscopy, videostroboscopy, voice assessment or other investigations. The exact tests depend on the suspected condition."},{"q":"Can vocal cord nodules or polyps be treated without surgery?","a":"Some vocal cord lesions may improve with voice rest, vocal hygiene, treatment of contributing conditions and professional voice therapy. Surgery may be considered when a structural lesion persists, significantly affects the voice or does not respond adequately to appropriate conservative treatment."},{"q":"What is LPR and can it affect the voice?","a":"Laryngopharyngeal reflux (LPR) occurs when stomach contents reach the throat or voice box and may contribute to throat clearing, cough, irritation, globus sensation or voice changes. Treatment is based on the overall clinical assessment and may include lifestyle measures and medicines when appropriate."},{"q":"What happens if a voice or throat problem is not treated on time?","a":"The consequences depend on the underlying condition. Some problems may settle without complications, while persistent inflammation, structural vocal cord lesions, swallowing disorders or airway problems may worsen or continue to affect communication, eating, sleep and quality of life. Persistent or unexplained symptoms should be evaluated rather than repeatedly self-treated."},{"q":"How long does voice and throat treatment take?","a":"Treatment time varies widely. Minor inflammatory conditions may improve within days to a few weeks, while voice therapy can require several weeks or longer. Recovery after endoscopic or laryngeal surgery depends on the procedure and individual healing. Your ENT specialist can provide a more specific timeline after diagnosis."},{"q":"How do I book a voice and throat consultation in Delhi?","a":"Call or WhatsApp Dr. Dhirendra Singh Kushwah's clinic at +91-9311449825, or email drdhirendrasinghent@gmail.com. Appointments are available at clinic locations in South Delhi by appointment."}]}]
